0|null@explainlikeimfive^1439990484#3hkxlh:zoechan$:ELI5: Why can you be involuntarily hospitalized for being suicidal?
1|0@explainlikeimfive^1439993476#cu890v2:2unique4suicide$:Suicide and self-mutilation arn't considered normal thought processes for a health human being. Once your sanity has been questioned, you loose the right to have any say in the matter. And the responsibility is now on the sane minds.
1|0@explainlikeimfive^1439993613#cu893nd:zoechan$:Self mutilation won't get you locked up though. Otherwise people with tattoos and piercings would be locked up. So either both should be or neither should be.
1|0@explainlikeimfive^1439994315#cu89iev:2unique4suicide$:Its a spectrum, i wouldn't consider those to be self mutilation under the definition. &gt;Self-mutilation, also called self-harm, self-injury or cutting, is the intentional destruction of tissue or alteration of the body done without the conscious wish to commit suicide , usually in an attempt to relieve tension. - &gt;The main issue of separating self-injurious acts from tattoos and piercings is that of pride. Most people who get tattooed and/or pierced are proud of their new decorations. They want to show others their ink, their studs, their plugs. They want to tell the story of the pain, the fear, the experience. In contrast, those who hurt themselves generally don't tell anyone about it. Self-injurers go to great lengths to cover and disguise their wounds and scars. Self-injurers are not proud of their new decorations.
1|0@explainlikeimfive^1439995686#cu8ac5z:zoechan$:Under the definition, it absolutely is self mutilation.
1|0@explainlikeimfive^1439996238#cu8aog0:2unique4suicide$:No, its about intent. Your not thinking of the damage your causing during these practices, your motivations aren't geared to the experience of the pain. Which makes it different...
1|0@explainlikeimfive^1439996507#cu8auhs:zoechan$:Mutilation: to injure, disfigure, or make imperfect by removing or irreparably damaging parts Nothing about intent here.
1|0@explainlikeimfive^1439997123#cu8b8c1:2unique4suicide$:That definition is so broad, that if i were to give myself a paper cut that would be considered self-mutilation? And i should be locked up for my own safety. Again intent is an important factor.
1|0@explainlikeimfive^1439997320#cu8bcs8:zoechan$:So then add intentional before. But either way, intentionally piercing one's ears or intentionally cutting oneself amount to the same thing. And my argument is that neither should be involuntarily committed. Basically the only reason people with tattoos and piercings aren't locked up is because they've become socially acceptable.
1|0@explainlikeimfive^1439998449#cu8c32a:2unique4suicide$:The intent of the individual isn't to hurt themselves during these practices. Self mutilation in the most definitive definition of mental health, takes intent into account. That's why courts have different degrees of murder convictions. Because even though the result is the same, intent is a factor. 
1|0@explainlikeimfive^1439998607#cu8c6v4:zoechan$:It depends on how you define "hurt."
1|0@explainlikeimfive^1439998805#cu8cbmp:2unique4suicide$:Either troll or extremist 
1|0@explainlikeimfive^1439999161#cu8ck9y:zoechan$:I guess extremist. After being held against my will in pretty fucking pissed.
1|0@explainlikeimfive^1439999646#cu8cw54:2unique4suicide$:They held you because they cared about your safety or the safety of others. Hows that a negative? Wouldn't you rather a person with serious mental issues get help, then turned away? 
1|0@explainlikeimfive^1439999879#cu8d1wy:zoechan$:How's that a negative? Let's see. They denied me my medication. Locked me in my room. Wouldn't let me call my family. Gave me food I couldn't eat. Forced me to participate in groups when I had a migraine. I wasn't suicidal when I went in but I sure as fuck was when I was there. And yes, a person with mental illness should get help. But ONLY if they WANT it.
6|0@explainlikeimfive^1439991791#cu88484:Arudin88$:The legal theory is based partially *parens patriae*, "parent of the fatherland," at it's most basic, the idea that the state is responsible for the safety of all of its citizens, particularly those that can't protect themselves for whatever reason. It is the basis for Child Protective Services, foster care systems, and (more relevantly) involuntary commitment. It's also part of how the state (both country and US state) gets its standing/right to sue others, like in criminal cases. And, like u/MultiFazed says, it's very much up for debate as to whether or not *you* want to commit suicide.
1|0@explainlikeimfive^1440049345#cu979tl:[deleted]$:[deleted]
1|0@explainlikeimfive^1440069026#cu9cjki:zoechan$:That's because he killed other people. That's completely different than someone shooting themselves or ODing or what have you.
2|0@explainlikeimfive^1439990673#cu87kdz:stuthulhu$:&gt;In the U.S., this seems like it should be illegal. Well, you have a recognized constitutional right to life. There's not one for death, to my knowledge. Hence, the right to do whatever you want to yourself would have to be enacted in law, and typically it hasn't been. I assume to some degree this is because suicide or self-mutilation have not been seen as valid life-choices, but more representative of mental illness or otherwise unjustifiable activities. 
2|0@explainlikeimfive^1439990976#cu87phv:zoechan$:But you also have the right to be free, unless you commit a crime. Being suicidal and attempting suicide aren't crimes. Yet you can be detained anyway.
3|0@explainlikeimfive^1439991030#cu87qff:stuthulhu$:Actually attempting suicide is a crime in some jurisdictions. 
1|0@explainlikeimfive^1439992045#cu8892a:zoechan$:It's not where I live, yet you can be locked up for it. But having suicidal urges isn't a crime, yet you can be locked up for those too.
1|0@explainlikeimfive^1439992296#cu88dow:m4xwellmurd3r$:It could be in the law that anyone that may be a danger to themselves or another may be detained. That way people who are mentally unstable that may inflict harm on themselves can be detained (whether its a voluntary action or not), but this would also apply to people trying to commit suicide.
16|0@explainlikeimfive^1439991012#cu87q3k:MultiFazed$:&gt;Why do you not have the right to do whatever you want to yourself, Because with suicide, it's very, *very* likely that it's **not** what you want for yourself, and is instead what your mental illness wants for you. Thus it is in your best interest to treat the underlying illness and *then* let you come back to that decision once it's believed that you are actually sound of mind, but that's exactly what you *won't* want to do because of the underlying mental illness, thus the involuntary treatment.
1|0@explainlikeimfive^1439991802#cu884gc:zoechan$:But you can't be forced to get treatment for any other type of illness. You have the right to refuse treatment for a terminal illness. So you should also have the right to refuse treatment for a mental illness, which by the way you do, *until* you are believed to be a danger to yourself. But cancer is always a danger, yet people have the right to let it kill them. Why do I not have the right to let my mental illness kill me?
1|0@explainlikeimfive^1439992349#cu88eqk:thepentexsucks$:You do not have the right to refuse treatment for mental illness that puts yourself in danger. Because you may not be able to make those sorts of decisions. We don't know enough about Mental illness to ascertain when a person is or isn't of sound mind with 100% accuracy. This is the correct way to do it to ensure that everyone has the highest chance of receiving the treatment that is right for them.
6|0@explainlikeimfive^1439992349#cu88eqw:MultiFazed$:&gt;Why do I not have the right to let my mental illness kill me? Because the mental illness prevents you from making rational decisions about whether or not you *actually* want to let the mental illness kill you. In short, you don't want to die. Rather, the illness makes you *think* that you want to die, and the illness should not be allowed to make life-or-death decisions for you. Imagine that someone had a physical illness that resulted in a coma followed by involuntary muscle twitches that caused them to slash at their wrists with their fingernails or something. Would you say, "Well, they clearly want to die, so why are you forcing them to take antibiotics instead of just letting them do what they want to themseves"? Of course not. And it's the same with mental illnesses. The only part that makes it hard to relate to is the fact that, while a physical illness may merely cause a change to someone's body (making it easy to say, "This isn't what they actually want"), a mental illness can causes changes to their **mind**, meaning that it looks like they honestly want to die, and they even believe so themselves. But they don't, and future-them will usually be immensely grateful if you prevent mentally-ill past-them from ending their life.
2|0@explainlikeimfive^1439993215#cu88vhz:zoechan$:&gt;In short, you don't want to die. Rather, the illness makes you *think* that you want to die This cannot be proven. And in any case, if the illness is causing me to suffer, and it has been untreatable, then why is the state going to force me to keep suffering? I can very clearly reason my way out of it. The illness isn't making me crazy. I'm perfectly capable of rational thought and I'm perfectly capable of discerning what's best for me. Just because I have a fancy label for it doesn't mean I'm not able to understand my predicament or my actions. &gt;But they don't, and future-them will usually be immensely grateful if you prevent mentally-ill past-them from ending their life But not always. And if so, then surely there are better ways of addressing it than by denying them due process or by traumatizing them in a mental hospital. My mental illness causes me pain. It does nothing else. It doesn't affect my thinking. Yet just because I want to die to end the pain people are allowed to tell me, as though they know better than me, that I don't really want to die. But how can they possibly know?
3|0@explainlikeimfive^1439994035#cu89cez:MultiFazed$:&gt;if the illness is causing me to suffer, and it has been untreatable, Then the rules are different. Untreatable mental illnesses are not dealt with the same way as treatable ones. &gt;I'm perfectly capable of rational thought and I'm perfectly capable of discerning what's best for me. Frankly, that's for doctors to decide, because every single person who *isn't* capable of rational thought, and *isn't* capable of discerning what's best for them, thinks that they are. That's the whole point here, actually. &gt;My mental illness causes me pain. It does nothing else. It doesn't affect my thinking. And as I said before, you're the least-qualified person to know whether or not your thinking is affected. Story time: My grandmother died of Alzheimer's. Early on, she knew that the Alzheimer's was getting worse, and she was taking various medications to help slow it down. But I remember one day when I was visiting her where she completely denied that she had any mental problems. When asked, "Well, why are you taking all this medication?" she responded that it was "to keep her healthy". At that point, not only did she not know that she had any mental problems, but she vehemently denied that it was even possible. So if someone has a mental illness that affects their thinking, of *course* they're not the best source to ask whether or not it affects their thinking. How would they even know? Thinking that has been affected by a mental illness just feels like normal thinking to the thinker.
2|0@explainlikeimfive^1439994362#cu89jfd:zoechan$:But there needs to be some sort of probable cause to determine whether or not it should be believed that a mentally ill person isn't thinking clearly. Alzheimer's is obvious. Depression is not. But you can't lock someone up for not thinking clearly.
2|0@explainlikeimfive^1439994925#cu89vg5:MultiFazed$:&gt;But there needs to be some sort of probable cause to determine whether or not it should be believed that a mentally ill person isn't thinking clearly. There is. That probable cause is, "This person is trying to harm/kill themselves." That's abnormal behavior, and in the absence of some other obvious cause (such as dying from pancreatic cancer or something) is a very strong indicator that there is some sort of mental illness affecting their thinking.
2|0@explainlikeimfive^1439995819#cu8af4v:zoechan$:My point is that there needs to be a way to determine if this abnormal behavior is done because a person isn't thinking clearly or if a person is coherent and simply desires to behave abnormally.
3|0@explainlikeimfive^1439995961#cu8aicj:MultiFazed$:&gt;My point is that there needs to be a way to determine if this abnormal behavior is done because a person isn't thinking clearly or if a person is coherent and simply desires to behave abnormally. Which is exactly what the involuntary hospitalization that you're complaining about is for in the first place. It's to give doctors a chance to evaluate the patient to determine the cause of their abnormal behavior without the patient killing themselves before such an evaluation can be completed.
1|0@explainlikeimfive^1439996086#cu8al2u:zoechan$:Except that it denies them due process. That's like jailing someone before they commit a crime. Not to mention, hospitals are notoriously traumatic and can end up making the illness worse.
1|0@explainlikeimfive^1439996619#cu8awyu:MultiFazed$:&gt;Except that it denies them due process. Yet it's the only valid choice. Because if you *don't* involuntarily commit suicidal people, then many, *many*, **many** people with treatable mental illnesses will kill themselves before their illness can be treated.
1|0@explainlikeimfive^1439997214#cu8babi:zoechan$:And they have the right to do so.
1|0@explainlikeimfive^1439997462#cu8bfzf:MultiFazed$:&gt;And they have the right to do so. I feel like you're missing the point, though. They don't *actually* want to kill themselves. Their mental illness is making the decisions instead of them. Their mental illness doesn't have the right to kill them if it can be treated instead. If you told me, "Hey, next year you're going to have a mental disorder that will cause you to want to commit suicide. It's totally treatable, but I'm just going to let you kill yourself instead," I'd beat the ever-loving crap out of you. That's the same as telling me that you know that I'm going to fall into a river and start drowning, and you plan on just watching me die.
1|0@explainlikeimfive^1439998236#cu8bxxx:zoechan$:But you can't know that they don't want to kill themselves. But you're taking their rights away anyway.
2|0@explainlikeimfive^1439998389#cu8c1ku:MultiFazed$:&gt; But you can't know that they don't want to kill themselves. Yes, we can. Just not immediately. It requires a thorough psychiatric evaluation. Essentially, the patient is two different people: The sane version of themselves, and the mentally-ill version of themselves. And mentally-ill-patient should not be allowed to essentially murder sane-patient unless it is determined that sane-patient can never be recovered with proper medical treatment. The involuntary commitment is there to see which of those two "versions" of the patient they're dealing with. &gt;But you're taking their rights away anyway. Mentally-ill-patient's rights are being taken away because sane-patient's rights are more important, and it needs to be determined whether or not sane-patient can be recovered.
0|0@explainlikeimfive^1439998573#cu8c610:zoechan$:Yet if a patient admits they're suicidal they're automatically seen as not sane. Then their rights can be taken away indefinitely.
3|0@explainlikeimfive^1440001379#cu8e2q4:shadow776$:&gt; Except that it denies them due process. &gt; That's like jailing someone before they commit a crime. It should be noted that the State can put you in jail for a period of time (usually up to 72 hours) for pretty much any or no reason. No charges, no due process. If involuntary psych hold was not a 'thing', they could still throw you in a jail cell for a few days instead. Most suicide attempts likely involve some kind of potential criminal charge, like a firearms charge, misuse of a controlled substance, public nuisance. 
1|0@explainlikeimfive^1440002332#cu8eqiz:zoechan$:&gt;It should be noted that the State can put you in jail for a period of time (usually up to 72 hours) for pretty much any or no reason. No charges, no due process. And that's unconstitutional.
1|0@explainlikeimfive^1440003146#cu8fb1l:shadow776$:Unfortunately it's not. Here's [one case](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/County_of_Riverside_v._McLaughlin) used to support the practice, there are likely others. To be clear, I personally agree with your position. But the reality is, the State can arrest and hold anyone it chooses, at least for a short time. And in practice, often for a very long time - a guy was jailed for 14 years on Contempt of Court charges which means no trial, no due process, no appeals. Just on the decision of a single Judge. 
1|0@explainlikeimfive^1440003809#cu8frrm:zoechan$:I'd argue it is, but that the constitution isn't being interpreted in the right way by those people. Legal and constitutional aren't the same thing in this case, though they should be in practice.
7|0@explainlikeimfive^1439991296#cu87v5a:thepentexsucks$:This Mental illness is a serious thing, if you are in a situation where you want to end your life, you are mentally ill - literally all of life on earth has had one common goal - to survive. Attempting to break that cycle for selfish means is a strong indication that something is wrong in your head.
1|0@explainlikeimfive^1439991883#cu885zi:zoechan$:&gt;Attempting to break that cycle for selfish means Isn't it more selfish that other people want that person around while they're suffering just for their own benefit?
-1|1@explainlikeimfive^1439992234#cu88ci9:thepentexsucks$:No, it is not.
5|0@explainlikeimfive^1439993449#cu890c4:NobbelGobble$:It's not that black and white. you have someone who is in incredible pain who is incapable of comprehending a future beyond that pain. His/Her loved ones believe that things can get better. If that person commits suicide because the pain is too much to bare anymore, they will be missed. But things will eventually get better for those who are still here.
2|0@explainlikeimfive^1439999049#cu8chjy:SporadicTendancies$:This. Chronic pain with no relief in sight. Some things are worth sticking around for, but when it gets bad the people that matter know when the only cure is death.
2|0@explainlikeimfive^1439992622#cu88jwo:zoechan$:How so? And why is suicide selfish?
2|0@explainlikeimfive^1440007489#cu8ifd8:Bobolequiff$:Your question was about being suicidal, now you're making it about euthanasia. You didn't specify suffering initially.
1|0@explainlikeimfive^1440007705#cu8ikzg:zoechan$:If someone is suicidal they're more than likely suffering.
4|0@explainlikeimfive^1440008849#cu8jeja:Bobolequiff$:Nope. Nopenopenope. If someone is suicidal, they mite than likely have a little voice in their head telling them that the tiny problem they're facing right now is insurmountable and life isn't worth living and they should just kill themselves. Source: Have been suicidal before and come close to topping myself over stuff I can now clearly see was bullshit. THAT'S what the hospitalisation I meant to combat. Suicide is normally a rash, spur of the moment decision, it's like buying that expensive thing you don't need and can't really afford; you either buy it immediately even though you know you shouldn't or you take a moment to think about it and come to your senses. The argument about euthanasia is not the same thing.
-2|0@explainlikeimfive^1440009213#cu8jnz1:zoechan$:That doesn't change the fact that it denies them due process. But maybe you're right. Maybe they should only hold people who have been suffering temporarily and not people like me who have been battling untreatable depression their entire lives.
